<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cheontae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1097</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Korean Tiantai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cheontae is the Korean form of the Tiantai school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, which centres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra</a>. It was established as a separate order in Goryeo in 1097 by the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uicheon">Uicheon</a>, who sought to reconcile doctrinal study with meditation and to unite the competing Buddhist schools. Revived in the twentieth century, it is today one of the largest Buddhist orders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cheontae.jpg" alt="Cheontae" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kernbeisser (a flickr user) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Joseon_Dynasty-Gilt-bronze_seated_Avalokitesvara_at_Beopjusa_Temple-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
